{
  "requirement": "Before presenting, the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to apply the revised text the reviews return. Where reviews conflict, for example two reviews that split the same requirement differently, it MUST direct them to reconcile the conflict, and to present as a design question any conflict whose resolution changes what is promised.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The requirement `requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill.present.apply` has no structural obligations. It says what the `habeas-requirements` skill must direct agents to do before presenting. Agents must apply the revised text the reviews return. They must reconcile conflicting reviews, such as two reviews that split the same requirement differently. They must present as a design question any conflict whose resolution changes what is promised. All three clauses are about the content of the skill's guidance. None names a library, algorithm, signature, type, field or named interface. None says how the guidance must be built. Any wording or placement in `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md` that gives these directions meets the text. Moving the directions to another section or rephrasing them would not violate any clause. No implementation root is needed beyond the existing `Implements` edge from `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md`. Whether the directions are present and adequate is a behavioral question, not a structural one. The outline I could read showed only elided text, so I did not check that.\n\nbehavioral: The requirement is about what the `habeas-requirements` skill text directs. It has three obligations: apply the revised text the reviews return before presenting, reconcile conflicting reviews, and present as a design question any conflict whose resolution changes what is promised.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_applies_and_reconciles_reviews_before_presenting]` is marked {test} and covers all three:\n- **Apply before presenting.** `step_of(SKILL, \"Apply the revised text the reviews return\")` must find a numbered workflow step containing that phrase. It panics if none does. The final `assert!(apply < step_of(SKILL, \"Present each group to the user\"))` checks that this step comes before the presenting step.\n- **Reconcile conflicts.** `assert_text` checks that the apply step contains \"reconcile reviews that clash, such as two that split the same requirement differently\". That covers the requirement's example of two reviews splitting a requirement differently.\n- **Design question.** `assert_text` checks that the same step contains \"a clash whose resolution changes what is promised becomes a design question\".\n\nI read the helpers `step`, `step_of` and `assert_text`. They search the skill's numbered workflow steps and assert on the step's text, so the assertions enforce the expected content. I saw no further observable obligation and no counterexample the test would accept.",
